-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Origin
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

PredatorCore is a modified version of Div Acer Manager Max (DAMX) by PXDiv,
https://github.com/PXDiv/Div-Acer-Manager-Max, which is licensed under the
GNU General Public License v3.0. The original authors retain copyright in their
work. Derived from upstream commit 8b2c736 (September 2026).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Changes made by ruskaruma (2026-09-29)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Renamed the project, namespace and binary to PredatorCore. New name, icon and
  branding. The upstream mascot artwork was removed.
- Redesigned the whole GUI in a PredatorSense-inspired style: sidebar
  navigation, status header, new theme and restyled controls.
- New Monitoring page with arc gauges and additional metrics: CPU clocks,
  package power, GPU clock, power, VRAM and power state, fan duty, memory,
  swap, disk, NVMe temperature, network, battery health, cycles and draw,
  uptime and load.
- New components: ArcGauge, KeyboardPreview (live 4-zone preview) and
  SystemMetricsSampler / MonitoringViewModel.
- Efficiency: polling is paused while the dashboard is hidden or minimised;
  the runtime-suspended NVIDIA GPU is not woken; a single nvidia-smi query
  replaces two; CPU usage no longer sleeps 100 ms per sample.
- Fixed the battery percentage binding being overwritten at startup.
- Battery time-left now handles charge_* batteries.
- Renamed DAMXClient/DAMXSettings to DaemonClient/DaemonSettings. The daemon
  socket path (/var/run/DAMX.sock), log path and config path are unchanged
  for compatibility with existing daemon installs.
- Daemon: added a provenance header and rebranded its CLI description. The
  daemon's behaviour is unchanged.
- Removed the upstream release workflows, remote installers, IDE files and docs.
  Added a local build/install script and a CI build workflow.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Changes made by ruskaruma (2026-09-30)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Daemon: new performance.py with CPU power limit (PL1/PL2) control across
  both RAPL interfaces (MSR and MMIO) and a fan curve engine (hysteresis,
  95 °C safety override, re-applies power limits after firmware profile
  changes). New commands: get/set/reset_power_limits, get/set_fan_curve.
  Manual fan speed changes disable the curve.
- GUI: CPU power limits card and a Curve fan mode with a drag-to-edit
  FanCurveEditor on the Performance page.
- GUI: "What's heating your laptop" process list (with a safe SIGTERM End
  button that is hidden for session-critical processes), "keeping the GPU
  awake" card, and thermal throttling indicator on the Monitoring page.
- GUI: temperature history now shows clock times and marks the latest reading.
- GUI: static FanDial graphic replaces the spinning fan icons; chart
  animations disabled; dashboard stops sampling while hidden. Idle CPU use
  dropped from about 20-28% of a core to about 3-5%.
- GUI: daemon replies are read in full (previously truncated at 4 KB).
- GUI: X11 WM_CLASS set so docks match the desktop entry; optional local
  artwork override in ~/.local/share/predatorcore/icons.
- New glass-style app icon generated by assets/make_icon.py; installer
  installs all icon sizes. New scripts/install-daemon.sh.

- Fixed: the dashboard and fan-curve loop polled nvidia-smi every 2 s, which
  kept the dGPU from ever runtime-suspending. The GPU is now read at most every
  15 s while awake, never while asleep, and GPU name/driver come from
  /proc and /sys instead of nvidia-smi/lspci (neither wakes the GPU).
- GPU power control (auto sleep / always on) via PCI runtime PM, in the daemon
  (get/set_gpu_power) and on the Monitoring page.
- Right-anchored temperature history (NOW at the right edge, gaps while the
  GPU sleeps); GPU gauges show SLEEP instead of 0.
- The GUI no longer re-sends an unchanged thermal profile on every settings
  reload (each write made the firmware reset its power limits).
- Glass theme: translucent window with tinted backdrop, translucent panels
  with lit edges, glossy buttons.

